% Problem 31. Chace vol. II pl. 53 (photograph XIII, registers 1-4; also photograph XV, register 4), PDF pp. 153-154; Peet pp. 66-67 (Pl. J).
% Order as in Chace (the papyrus's). Peet rearranges: the column, its total; then the '7 '8 '14 '28 '28 line, 17 '4, 3 '2 '4; then the 42 column; then the '97 column and "Total 33" (Peet: "Step 2 ... in the papyrus is misplaced after Step 4").
No. 31
{"A quantity, 2/3 of it, '2 of it, '7 of it, added to it, becomes it: 33."}
  1      1 2/3 '2 '7
\ 2      4 '3 '4 '28
\ 4      9 6 '18          % 6 for '6: Chace fn 1: "The scribe omitted the fractional dots in these places."; Peet: "'18 (read '14)"; Chace prints '18 without comment
\ 8      18 '3 '7
  '2     '2 '3 '4 '14
\ '4     '4 6 '8 '28      % 6 for '6 (Chace fn 1)
"The total is 32 '2, the remainder is '2."   % 32 '2 is the whole numbers and larger fractions only (Peet: "though the scribe when he writes 'Total 32 '2' gives no hint of this"); the ticked multipliers make 14 '4
% beside it, on the right: 1 2/3 '2 '7 in 42nds. Chace: "The calculation of 97 (incorrectly written 99) is placed in the papyrus at the end of Problem 38, but belongs here"; Peet sets it here too, and again in No. 33
  1      42
  2/3    28
  '2     21
  '7     6
Total 99.                 % Chace: "incorrectly written 99" (for 97); Peet: "99 (read 97)"
% the remainder divided: each row is multiplier, its product on 1 2/3 '2 '7, and that product in 42nds
\ '97              '42     1
\ '56 '679 '776    21      2     % 21 for '21 (Chace fn 1); Peet '21
\ '194             '84     '2
\ '388             '168    '4
"'7 '8 '14 '28 '28"       % the fractions of the column left out of 32 '2
"6 5 '4 3 1 '2 1 '2"      % beneath them, their values in 42nds (black in Chace)
"Total 33."               % "dmd 33", written here beneath the line above; Peet: "the words 'Total 33,' misplaced in the papyrus"
"17 '4"                   % the sum of 6 5 '4 3 1 '2 1 '2
"3 '2 '4"                 % what 17 '4 lacks of 21 (Peet: "<Remainder> 3 '2 + '4")
"'2 21"                   % '2 is 21 (in 42nds) (Peet: "(since) '2 is 21")
% Chace: "The calculations by which 17 '4, and then 3 '2 '4 are obtained belong directly after the result of the first multiplication."
